\title{paindrawr: Quantification of pain drawings}
\description{
This package aims to aid in the quantification of pain drawings. Pain drawings are typically used in a clinical context, e.g. low back pain, where patients are invited to illustrate the pain distribution on a schematic outline of the human body. Historically, these pain drawings were completed using pen and paper with a body silhouette pre-printed. Today, they are typically collected using digital interfaces like a webpage or an app on a tablet. There are basically two methods of collecting such data. Either as simple categorical data (did the patient draw a pain area including the 'left lower back'? yes or no, and similarly for other anatomical regions). This approach was (among others) published by Margolis several decades ago and lends itself well to pen-and-paper pain drawings. Another approach which is more easily recorded using digital interfaces, is to capture the actual pen strokes as series of x and y coordinate data. This is also more difficult to manage and in some cases interpret.
}
